WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:11.100
In autumn 2023, researchers ran an experiment with nearly 1,000 students at a high school in Turkey.

2
00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:19.770
One group could ask an AI anything while practising; another used an AI that gave hints, not answers; a third used no AI.

3
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:25.960
During practice, the group with unrestricted AI improved their scores by 48%.

4
00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:34.750
But in an exam without AI, they did 17% worse than students who had never used AI.

5
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:44.070
In the hints-only group, that harm was largely cancelled out. And the students didn't notice they had learned less.

6
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:52.347
This is the last episode of the core series, C8: when not to use AI, and staying an independent learner.

7
00:00:52.697 --> 00:00:57.697
It's not that AI is bad; some skills need to live in you.

8
00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:04.587
That study has limits too: one school, one subject, short-term effects.

9
00:01:04.937 --> 00:01:15.857
A survey of 319 knowledge workers found that more confidence in AI went with less reported critical thinking. But it's self-reported.

10
00:01:16.207 --> 00:01:25.767
And in a brain-scan experiment, people who wrote essays with a large model struggled most to quote what they had just written. But it's a preprint with a small sample.

11
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:33.997
So write the conclusion as 'in this study', not 'AI makes people stupid'.

12
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:39.207
So when shouldn't you use it? Ask yourself six questions.

13
00:01:39.757 --> 00:01:45.437
Am I learning this skill? Does it need my judgement and responsibility? Is it something between people?

14
00:01:45.787 --> 00:01:52.507
Is it allowed? Does it involve privacy or confidentiality? Does the result matter, and I can't check it?

15
00:01:52.857 --> 00:01:59.777
If you're learning a skill, do it yourself first; if you need help, ask AI for hints, not answers.

16
00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:09.607
Tool one: the AI-off checkpoint. Every so often, switch AI off and do the same kind of task yourself.

17
00:02:09.957 --> 00:02:19.917
A demo: a university student writes a lab report with AI off. She can describe the data, but can't explain what the results mean.

18
00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:27.347
So she changes how she uses it: she writes three key points first, then asks AI only to point out gaps in the logic.

19
00:02:27.697 --> 00:02:40.777
Tool two: the reflection log. After each use, write five lines: what I did, which part was mine, could I do it with AI off, what I learned, what I'll do myself next time.

20
00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:44.167
Finally, let's tie the eight lessons together.

21
00:02:44.517 --> 00:02:56.437
C1, talking isn't taking responsibility; C2, why it makes things up; C3, explain the task clearly; C4, verify according to risk.

22
00:02:56.787 --> 00:03:10.427
C5, hold the three red lines; C6, check for bias and think through your work; C7, set checkpoints and keep a supervision log; C8, know when to do it yourself.

23
00:03:10.777 --> 00:03:17.017
One through-line: verify first, then supervise, and finally learn when not to use it.

24
00:03:17.617 --> 00:03:31.297
Three badges: L1 'Know', pass the practical quizzes; L2 'Use', hand in a task product; L3 'Verify', a verification challenge and supervision log, plus an oral defence.

25
00:03:32.067 --> 00:03:37.507
Use it, check it, supervise it, and know when to do it yourself.

26
00:03:38.107 --> 00:03:44.547
Quick exercise: pick one skill and set your first AI-off checkpoint.

27
00:03:44.897 --> 00:03:50.457
That's the end of the AI Literacy for Everyone core series. Thank you for watching all the way through.
